  "title": {
    "en": "Chats, flycatchers and Ring Ouzel"
  },
  "subtitle": {
    "en": "Redstart, Black Redstart, Whinchat, Stonechat, Wheatear, Spotted and Pied Flycatcher and Ring Ouzel"
  },
  "intro": {
    "en": "Chats and flycatchers are birds of the spring and autumn migration. They perch in the open, on a post or a twig, and fly out for insects. Most of the birds in this lesson are long-distance migrants, going to Africa for winter, and they pass through north-west Europe in April to May and August to October. Autumn falls, when a night of east wind brings thousands to the coast, are the best days for them.\n\nThe tail is the key for many: a Redstart has a rusty tail that it quivers, a Wheatear has a white rump and a black T on the tail, a Black Redstart has a dark tail with a rusty edge. The Whinchat has white at the base of the tail and a strong eyebrow, the Stonechat has a dark head and a white neck patch. For flycatchers, look at the way they sit and fly out to catch a fly.\n\nMales in spring are bright and easy. In autumn, males, females and young are all in a dull plumage, and the pro skill is to read what is left."
  },
  "words": [
    {
      "term": {
        "en": "rump"
      },
      "explain": {
        "en": "The lower back, just above the tail. White in Wheatear, a white patch in a Stonechat."
      }
    },
    {
      "term": {
        "en": "supercilium"
      },
      "explain": {
        "en": "The pale stripe above the eye. Strong in a Whinchat and weak in a Stonechat."
      }
    },
    {
      "term": {
        "en": "wing panel"
      },
      "explain": {
        "en": "A pale patch on the folded wing. Pied Flycatcher males have a white one."
      }
    },
    {
      "term": {
        "en": "gorget"
      },
      "explain": {
        "en": "A band or patch on the throat, often a different colour from the breast. A Ring Ouzel has a white one."
      }
    }
  ],
